          No protein powder builds muscle on its own. Training builds muscle; protein, eaten in enough total quantity alongside enough calories, lets your body keep and grow what training starts. A powder earns its place when it is the cheapest, easiest way to close the gap between what you eat and roughly 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight a day. Choose one that delivers 25-40 g of protein and a solid dose of leucine per shake, at a cost you can sustain for months, and the brand matters far less than the label suggests.
        </p>

