        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Which Powder to Buy for Muscle
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you lift and want one tub, buy <strong>Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey</strong>. It delivers about 24 g of protein per scoop, per the label at the time of writing, from a whey blend that naturally carries roughly 2.5 g or more of leucine per scoop, the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle protein synthesis. It usually sits at the cheap end of the mid-price tier per 25 g of protein, and Optimum Nutrition states that it carries Informed Choice certification. Two scoops after a hard session give you a dose the research supports; one scoop with a light meal tops up a day that falls short.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other picks answer more specific problems. If money is tight, Myprotein Impact Whey on sale is among the cheapest major-brand wheys per gram of protein. If you are cutting, lactose-sensitive or simply want the most protein per calorie, Dymatize ISO100 is a hydrolyzed and filtered isolate at about 25 g of protein per scoop. If you want a slow protein before bed, Gold Standard 100% Casein does that job. If you avoid dairy, Garden of Life Sport is a certified plant blend at about 30 g per serving. If you cannot eat enough to gain weight, Optimum Nutrition Pro Gainer adds real calories with far more protein than a classic mass gainer. And if you are drug tested, Thorne Whey Protein Isolate carries NSF Certified for Sport.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lifter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Our pick</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Most lifters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 g protein, mid-price, Informed Choice claimed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tight budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Myprotein Impact Whey Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low cost per 25 g of protein, especially on sale</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cutting or lactose-sensitive</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dymatize ISO100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g protein with very little lactose, fat or carbs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nighttime or long gaps</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow-digesting, thick, about 24 g protein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plant-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Garden of Life Sport Plant-Based Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g protein, NSF Certified for Sport</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hardgainers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Optimum Nutrition Pro Gainer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 650 calories and 60 g protein per full serving</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drug-tested lifters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thorne Whey Protein Isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Before any of that, check whether you need a powder at all. If you already eat around 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight from food, a shake adds convenience rather than muscle. Run your numbers through our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> first. Plenty of lifters discover they are closer than they thought, and plenty discover they are 40-60 g a day short, which is exactly the gap one or two shakes fill without any change to how you cook.
          </p>

          <p>
            This page is about protein for building muscle. If your real problem is that the scale will not move at all, the calorie side matters more than the protein side, and our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">best protein powder for weight gain</a> covers mass gainers in depth. For rankings across every goal, including fat loss and general health, see our main <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-drives-gain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Drives Muscle Gain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Muscle growth has a clear order of importance, and protein powder sits near the bottom of it. First comes training: progressive resistance training, taken close enough to failure, often enough, for months. Without that stimulus, extra protein is simply burned for energy or stored. Second comes total daily protein. Third comes total calories, which decide whether your body has the material and energy to build new tissue. Only after those three do the form of protein, its timing or the brand start to matter, and even then the effects are small.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Total protein is the lever</h3>

          <p>
            The best single summary of the evidence is the 2018 meta-analysis led by Robert Morton in the British Journal of Sports Medicine, which pooled 49 randomized trials of protein supplementation during resistance training. Supplementing added modest extra gains in fat-free mass and strength, and the benefit plateaued at around 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, with the upper end of the confidence interval near 2.2 g/kg. Above that, more protein did not buy measurably more muscle. Our guide on whether <a href="/do-protein-supplements-build-muscle/">protein supplements build muscle</a> walks through that study in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical reading is that a powder works by raising your daily total, not through anything special in the scoop. The International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand on protein lands in the same place, recommending 1.4-2.0 g/kg per day for people who train. For an 80 kg lifter, 1.6 g/kg is about 128 g of protein a day. If your food supplies 90 g, one or two shakes close the gap. If your food already supplies 140 g, the shake is mostly expensive convenience. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> has the full table by body weight and goal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leucine per dose</h3>

          <p>
            Within that daily total, each meal or shake works best when it carries enough protein to fully trigger muscle protein synthesis. The ISSN puts the useful dose at roughly 20-40 g of protein, or about 0.4 g/kg, containing around 2-3 g of leucine, the amino acid that acts as the main on-switch for the process. Whey is roughly 10-11% leucine, so a 25 g scoop clears the threshold comfortably. Most plant proteins sit closer to 7-9%, which is why plant shakes work best at 30 g or more per serving.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Calories</h3>

          <p>
            Building muscle is far easier in a modest calorie surplus. A 2019 review of off-season bodybuilding nutrition by Iraki, Helms and colleagues suggested aiming for slow weight gain of roughly 0.25-0.5% of body weight per week, enough to support new tissue without piling on fat. Beginners and people with more body fat can build muscle at maintenance or even in a small deficit, but experienced lean lifters who stay in a deficit rarely add much size, however much protein they drink.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
