        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run a lab or a taste panel. Every pick here comes from label analysis, published research on protein quality and the certification status each brand publicly states, the same method behind every roundup on the site, described in full on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page. We only considered long-established products that have been sold for years by brands that publish a full nutrition panel and amino acid information.
          </p>

          <p>
            Four criteria did most of the work. The first was protein per serving and protein density: how much of each scoop is protein rather than carbohydrate, fat or filler, because that drives how many grams you get for your money. The second was leucine and amino acid quality, which favors dairy proteins and well-built plant blends over single-source plant powders. The third was cost per 25 g of protein at typical retail prices, because muscle is built over many months and a powder you can only afford for one of them does very little. The fourth was third-party certification from NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification matters more in this category than in most. Powders marketed to lifters sit next to pre-workouts and so-called test boosters on the same shelves, and the supplement industry has a long record of products found to contain substances that are not on the label. A banned-substance certification does not make a powder more effective, but it does mean an independent body screens batches for prohibited compounds. If you compete in a tested federation, it is not optional. Certifications attach to specific products and sometimes specific flavors, and listings change, so confirm the exact tub in the certifier's own database before you buy. Our guide to whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> covers heavy metals and contamination in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            We marked down powders that hide protein per serving inside a proprietary blend, that inflate the protein figure with cheap added amino acids such as glycine or taurine, a practice known as amino spiking, or that use serving sizes so small the numbers look better than they are. We also excluded anything leaning on hormone, testosterone or anabolic claims. A protein powder is food. Any tub promising drug-like effects is either overselling or should worry you, and neither is a good sign for what else is inside.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whey-casein-blends" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Why Whey, Casein and Blends Lead for Muscle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dairy proteins dominate this list for reasons that show up in the research, not just in marketing. Whey is digested quickly, carries the most leucine of any common protein source and scores at the top of protein quality measures such as DIAAS. In acute laboratory studies it produces a sharp, high rise in muscle protein synthesis after a workout. Casein, the other main milk protein, forms a gel in the stomach and releases amino acids over several hours, giving a lower but longer response that suits long gaps between meals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over weeks and months, the speed difference matters less than people think. Long-term training studies comparing whey with casein, or with blends of the two, generally show similar muscle gains when total daily protein is matched. That is why a whey blend such as Gold Standard, which mixes isolate and concentrate, works as well for muscle as a more expensive pure isolate. The isolate earns its premium only if lactose gives you trouble or every calorie counts, as our comparison of <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already know dairy suits you and want to compare more options, our guide to the <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> ranks whey products on their own. For most lifters, whey is the default because it combines the highest leucine content, the deepest research base and some of the lowest prices per gram of protein in the category. That combination is hard to beat for anyone trying to build muscle on a budget.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can plant protein build muscle?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, with two adjustments. Plant proteins are usually lower in leucine and sometimes in other essential amino acids, so they work best as blends, with pea and rice the common pairing, and at slightly larger doses of around 30-40 g. Studies comparing pea or soy protein with whey during resistance training have generally found similar muscle gains when the dose was adequate, though that evidence base is smaller than for whey. If you avoid dairy, a certified plant blend at a full serving is a sound choice rather than a compromise. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> comparison has the details.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen is the protein to skip for this purpose. It is low in leucine and contains no tryptophan at all, which makes it an incomplete protein, so a scoop of collagen counts for far less toward muscle than its gram number suggests. It may have uses for connective tissue, but it should not count toward your muscle-building protein total, and a powder that blends in a lot of collagen to raise its protein figure deserves a closer look at the label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dose-per-shake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Protein per Shake for Muscle
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most lifters, 25-40 g of protein per shake is the useful range. Below about 20 g, a single dose may not fully trigger muscle protein synthesis in a young adult, and the shortfall is larger for older lifters and for plant proteins. One scoop of most whey powders, at 20-25 g, sits at the bottom of the range; two scoops put you near the top. Which you choose depends on body size, the size of the meal the shake replaces and how much protein the rest of your day delivers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The old idea that the body can only use 20-30 g of protein at a time does not survive scrutiny. Early studies showed muscle protein synthesis plateaued around 20 g of whey after a leg workout, but later work by Macnaughton and colleagues found 40 g produced a larger response than 20 g after a whole-body session, and a 2023 study from Maastricht University found that 100 g of protein produced a larger and longer-lasting anabolic response than 25 g. Larger doses are not wasted; they are used over more hours. Spreading protein across three to five meals is still a sensible default, but a big shake is not thrown away.
          </p>

