        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the shake to do the training</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A protein powder supplies material, not stimulus. Without progressive resistance training, extra protein simply gets burned for energy or stored. If your lifts have not improved in months, look at your program, effort and consistency before switching powders. No scoop compensates for training that stopped challenging your muscles, however impressive its label looks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring your daily total</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drinking one shake after training while eating 70 g of protein the rest of the day leaves most lifters well short of the roughly 1.6 g/kg where gains plateau. The daily total is what drives results. Count protein for a few days, find the gap, and size your shakes to fill it rather than following the label serving.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Under-dosing plant protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plant proteins usually carry less leucine than whey, so a 20 g scoop of pea protein is a weaker signal than 20 g of whey. If you use plant protein, pick a blend and use a full serving of 30 g or more. That small change brings plant shakes close to whey for building muscle at only modest extra cost.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a mass gainer when you need protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Classic mass gainers are mostly carbohydrate, often 1,000 calories or more per serving with a modest share of protein. If you already gain weight easily or have body fat to lose, that is the wrong product. Gainers suit lifters who genuinely cannot eat enough; everyone else does better with plain whey and real food.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for recovery extras</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Added BCAAs, glutamine and enzyme blends raise the price without adding meaningful muscle for people who already eat enough protein, and whey already contains plenty of BCAAs. Spend the difference on more total protein, a tub of creatine monohydrate or better food. The best recovery formula is still sleep, adequate protein and manageable training volume.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

